The aesthetic you have here also works surprisingly well because the watercolor/camo backdrop feels slightly incongruous with sterile EXPERIMENT 0047 language. I’d preserve that tension: natural/feral imagery + clinical/research language + absurd internet behavior.

That begins creating an actual Wild Hawg grammar:

wild ↔ controlled
human ↔ machine
pig ↔ hawg
advertisement ↔ experiment
observer ↔ subject
brand ↔ audience

And the recurring transformation is always toward REWILD.

So I wouldn’t open with “Hey, we’re Wild Hawg, an energy gummy company.”

I’d open with 0047.

Make people wonder what the hell happened during the first 46 experiments.

And never completely tell them.